1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,560
The school assigns suction depth and tube-displacement learning. The
goal is to reason safely through airway danger, not to memorize a
universal tube-change recipe.

2
00:00:10,560 --> 00:00:21,576
Start with responsiveness, effective breathing and immediate
ventilation needs. A long device inventory must not delay support for
apnea or severe distress.

3
00:00:21,576 --> 00:00:29,520
Ask the patient or trained caregiver what is normal, where the
emergency plan is and which equipment is personal to this airway.

4
00:00:29,520 --> 00:00:38,736
This lesson prepares for a supervised school station. It does not
certify suctioning or authorize routine replacement of a tracheostomy
tube.

5
00:00:38,736 --> 00:00:48,048
A tracheostomy is an opening into the trachea. The patient may have
variable upper-airway patency depending on the indication and anatomy.

6
00:00:48,048 --> 00:00:57,216
After total laryngectomy, the airway is separated from the mouth and
nose. Ventilation through the face may not reach the lungs.

7
00:00:57,216 --> 00:01:04,752
A cricothyrotomy is created at a different anatomical site and has
different equipment and training requirements.

8
00:01:04,752 --> 00:01:16,536
Ask about the operation, device and usual breathing route; look for
reliable records or a patient-specific plan. Never label an airway
from a quick visual assumption alone.

9
00:01:16,536 --> 00:01:26,880
Some tracheostomy patients can breathe around the tube and through the
upper airway; others cannot. Anatomy may also change with swelling or
illness.

10
00:01:26,880 --> 00:01:36,072
A patient with total laryngectomy depends on the stoma for
ventilation. Face-mask oxygen alone does not solve a blocked stoma.

11
00:01:36,072 --> 00:01:43,008
A speaking valve, cap, cuff, upper-airway obstruction and altered
anatomy all affect how air moves.

12
00:01:43,008 --> 00:01:51,576
Observe chest movement, airflow and response to support; do not assume
that a visible stoma proves the tube is patent.

13
00:01:51,576 --> 00:02:01,056
Not every tracheostomy has an inner cannula, and designs differ.
Identify the actual model before attempting to remove any component.

14
00:02:01,056 --> 00:02:10,608
The cuff is not a one-way valve and does not reliably prevent
aspiration. Its status affects ventilation, leakage and comfort.

15
00:02:10,608 --> 00:02:20,952
A removed inner cannula may also remove the standard ventilation
connector on some devices. Follow the actual device design and
emergency plan.

16
00:02:20,952 --> 00:02:32,760
Do not inflate, deflate or remove a cuff simply because a textbook
lists that step. The indication, training, device and current local
procedure must all match.

17
00:02:32,760 --> 00:02:41,688
The obstruction may be at a humidification filter, speaking valve,
cap, connector or oxygen tubing rather than deep in the airway.

18
00:02:41,688 --> 00:02:51,024
Ask the patient or caregiver what is normally attached and what was
recently changed. A device may be essential to a home ventilator
setup.

19
00:02:51,024 --> 00:02:58,896
Check visible connections, kinks and equipment function without
disconnecting a ventilator or device beyond your training.

20
00:02:58,896 --> 00:03:06,480
Remove an attachment only when its purpose is understood and the
patient-specific plan or trained procedure supports it.

21
00:03:06,480 --> 00:03:17,640
Assess airway and breathing immediately. Look for poor chest rise,
increased effort, noisy airflow, cyanosis or a falling level of
responsiveness.

22
00:03:17,640 --> 00:03:26,784
Supplemental oxygen does not correct ineffective ventilation. Use the
appropriate trained support pathway when breaths are inadequate.

23
00:03:26,784 --> 00:03:36,864
Use monitoring as an aid, not a substitute for the patient. A normal-
looking number does not overrule a deteriorating level of effort or
consciousness.

24
00:03:36,864 --> 00:03:44,160
Ask another team member to prepare suction and the patient-specific
backup equipment while primary support continues.

25
00:03:44,160 --> 00:03:53,904
Ask the patient directly where possible. Some people with a
tracheostomy communicate by voice, writing, gesture or an assistive
device.

26
00:03:53,904 --> 00:04:03,816
A trained caregiver may know the baseline, exact tube model, usual
suction depth and the home emergency plan. Confirm, do not assume.

27
00:04:03,816 --> 00:04:11,016
Locate the personal emergency kit and check that the identified
equipment is actually available and compatible.

28
00:04:11,016 --> 00:04:20,472
Separate the first noticed change from the presumed cause. The family
may report a plug, but the patient still needs an independent airway
assessment.

29
00:04:20,472 --> 00:04:29,184
Check the suction source and tubing before the procedure. A powered
unit that is not charged or connected is not a working backup.

30
00:04:29,184 --> 00:04:39,264
Select the catheter using the actual tube dimensions and applicable
training guidance. A smaller catheter is not automatically correct for
every tube.

31
00:04:39,264 --> 00:04:50,184
Use a premeasured tube-limited insertion depth or the patient-specific
written depth. Never estimate an unseen carina distance from a generic
nursing diagram.

32
00:04:50,184 --> 00:05:00,120
Have oxygenation, monitoring and a plan for deterioration available.
The patient can desaturate or become bradycardic during an airway
procedure.

33
00:05:00,120 --> 00:05:09,360
Suction may be considered when secretions are visible, audible or
associated with impaired ventilation and the patient cannot clear them
effectively.

34
00:05:09,360 --> 00:05:19,224
A trained patient may recognize a familiar mucus-plug pattern early.
Treat that history seriously while independently confirming current
findings.

35
00:05:19,224 --> 00:05:31,368
Routine suction can cause distress, mucosal trauma, bleeding,
bronchospasm or hypoxia. A schedule alone is not enough reason to
perform it in an emergency scene.

36
00:05:31,368 --> 00:05:39,744
Explain the procedure, prepare the equipment and oxygenation plan, and
use the least invasive effective action allowed by training.

37
00:05:39,744 --> 00:05:50,280
The school specifically names suction depth. The safest transferable
concept is to measure the actual tube or use a current patient-
specific depth plan before insertion.

38
00:05:50,280 --> 00:06:00,048
Mark the catheter at the measured endpoint so the operator can see the
stopping limit. Do not push to resistance and then withdraw by a
guessed amount.

39
00:06:00,048 --> 00:06:11,376
Advance gently without applying suction during insertion. If the
catheter does not pass to the expected tube-limited depth, stop and
reassess obstruction or displacement.

40
00:06:11,376 --> 00:06:19,872
A fixed number from an unrelated adult or nursing source is not a
universal depth for a child, different tube or altered stoma.

41
00:06:19,872 --> 00:06:29,184
Use the exact technique taught for the actual device and school
station. This is a supervised skill, not a procedure to improvise from
a slide.

42
00:06:29,184 --> 00:06:37,728
Do not apply continuous suction while advancing. Advance only to the
visible depth mark and do not force through resistance.

43
00:06:37,728 --> 00:06:46,656
Apply suction during withdrawal if that is the station procedure. Keep
the pass short and maintain observation of the patient throughout.

44
00:06:46,656 --> 00:06:55,536
Reoxygenate and reassess between passes. Repeated attempts without a
new assessment can worsen hypoxia and cause injury.

45
00:06:55,536 --> 00:07:05,856
After the pass, compare the same signs used before it: work of
breathing, chest movement, air entry, oxygenation and alertness.

46
00:07:05,856 --> 00:07:15,096
If no secretion is recovered or breathing does not improve, do not
assume the airway is clear. Recheck the device and consider other
causes.

47
00:07:15,096 --> 00:07:22,248
If the patient deteriorates, stop the procedure and move to the
trained airway-support and escalation plan.

48
00:07:22,248 --> 00:07:33,264
Record the reason for suction, measured insertion limit, response and
any complication. Avoid recording a routine action when the depth or
response is unknown.

49
00:07:33,264 --> 00:07:43,896
Failure to pass can reflect a blocked tube, displaced tube, kink,
secretions or incorrect equipment. It is not proof of only one cause.

50
00:07:43,896 --> 00:07:53,808
Stop rather than force the catheter. Force can injure tissue or create
a false passage, especially when the tract is new or the tube is
displaced.

51
00:07:53,808 --> 00:08:04,104
Check only those removable parts you are trained and authorized to
handle. A missing connector or partially displaced outer tube can be
mistaken for obstruction.

52
00:08:04,104 --> 00:08:14,352
Prioritize adequate ventilation, request advanced assistance and
transport. Do not spend repeated minutes attempting an unapproved tube
change.

53
00:08:14,352 --> 00:08:24,312
A recently created stoma may not have a stable tract. Replacing a tube
through a fresh or uncertain opening can create a false passage.

54
00:08:24,312 --> 00:08:34,440
Watch for a changed flange position, loss of expected airflow, leak,
subcutaneous air or sudden distress after movement or coughing.

55
00:08:34,440 --> 00:08:42,432
Do not blindly push a displaced tube back. Stop and use the patient-
specific emergency plan with trained assistance.

56
00:08:42,432 --> 00:08:54,144
The actual response depends on anatomy, stoma age, device, training,
service scope and patient response; this lesson cannot substitute for
that authorized algorithm.

57
00:08:54,144 --> 00:09:02,952
A face mask may help a person with a tracheostomy if the upper airway
remains patent; it may fail when the upper route is obstructed.

58
00:09:02,952 --> 00:09:10,344
For total laryngectomy, ventilation must be directed to the stoma
because the upper airway is disconnected.

59
00:09:10,344 --> 00:09:19,224
Cuff inflation, deflation and tube removal change the ventilation
route and require the actual device plan and qualified operator.

60
00:09:19,224 --> 00:09:30,096
Assess visible chest movement and air leak with every attempt. Do not
interpret a tight mask seal or a numerical reading as proof of
effective ventilation.

61
00:09:30,096 --> 00:09:37,656
DOPE can help structure troubleshooting when a ventilated patient
suddenly worsens. It is a reminder list, not a diagnosis.

62
00:09:37,656 --> 00:09:45,816
Check displacement and obstruction while support continues. Examine
connections and equipment without losing the airway.

63
00:09:45,816 --> 00:09:54,696
Pneumothorax and other clinical causes still require assessment in
context. A tracheostomy does not make every decline a tube fault.

64
00:09:54,696 --> 00:10:04,704
Use the list to prompt the next check, then reassess whether the
patient improves. A memorable mnemonic is not a substitute for a
current service pathway.

65
00:10:04,704 --> 00:10:14,472
A five-year-old with an established tracheostomy becomes less
interactive. The caregiver reports that breathing has become noisy
over several minutes.

66
00:10:14,472 --> 00:10:24,768
The crew sees secretions at the opening and checks chest rise, oxygen
equipment and the patient-specific plan. One member maintains the
primary assessment.

67
00:10:24,768 --> 00:10:37,512
For the classroom branch, the station supervisor supplies the measured
suction depth and appropriate catheter. The learner demonstrates a
brief, gentle, marked-depth pass on a manikin.

68
00:10:37,512 --> 00:10:48,192
The mannequin improves only after a small secretion is cleared. State
the reassessment findings, what would trigger escalation and what you
would report to the receiving team.

69
00:10:48,192 --> 00:10:55,008
Report whether the patient normally uses the stoma, mouth or both for
breathing and how they usually communicate.

70
00:10:55,008 --> 00:11:03,432
Give the time of change, witnessed symptoms and relevant device event.
Distinguish direct observation from caregiver report.

71
00:11:03,432 --> 00:11:13,728
Describe the equipment checked, whether a catheter passed, the
measured depth used and the response. Do not collapse the sequence to
“suctioned, stable.”

72
00:11:13,728 --> 00:11:21,912
Include the patient-specific plan, caregiver expertise, equipment sent
with the patient and any requested advanced follow-up.

73
00:11:21,912 --> 00:11:32,472
The assigned nursing chapters discuss specimen collection, assessment
and tube care. Those chapters are educational sources, not paramedic
scope documents.

74
00:11:32,472 --> 00:11:42,912
Some emergency tube changes are designed for specifically trained
family caregivers with a personal plan. That does not authorize an
untrained responder to imitate them.

75
00:11:42,912 --> 00:11:53,472
Use the actual BC service procedure, school skill sheet, device
directions and instructor when the required depth, pressure or route
is not stated here.

76
00:11:53,472 --> 00:12:02,808
Do not use this slide deck as a bedside operational checklist. The
patient and local protocol take priority over a generic classroom aid.

77
00:12:02,808 --> 00:12:09,528
First state the airway anatomy and normal route, using confirmed
history rather than guessing from the stoma.

78
00:12:09,528 --> 00:12:18,576
Next name the observed problem and immediate support need. Avoid
diagnosing a mucus plug just because the caregiver used that phrase.

79
00:12:18,576 --> 00:12:27,264
If suction is indicated, explain how the actual depth limit is
obtained and how you will stop if resistance or deterioration occurs.

80
00:12:27,264 --> 00:12:36,384
Finally name the trained clinician, local service pathway and
escalation resource. If one is missing, do not invent the missing
permission.
